<div class="bhd-page__keypoints" aria-label="Five electrolyte species at a glance">
  <div><strong>Different compartments</strong><span>Sodium and chloride predominate outside cells, while potassium is concentrated mainly inside cells.</span></div>
  <div><strong>Different functions</strong><span>Electrolytes contribute to osmotic balance, electrical signalling, membrane transport and acid-base regulation.</span></div>
  <div><strong>One formula system</strong><span>In Best Hydrate, the five highlighted species come from sodium chloride, potassium chloride, sodium bicarbonate and citric-acid chemistry.</span></div>
</div>

<section class="bhd-page__cards-section">
  <p class="bhd-page__kicker">The five species we highlight</p>
  <h2>Same word, very different jobs</h2>
  <div class="bhd-page__cards bhd-page__cards--4">
    <article><h3>Sodium, Na<sup>+</sup></h3><p>The principal extracellular cation. Sodium is central to extracellular fluid osmolality and volume and participates in nerve signalling and intestinal cotransport.</p><a href="/pages/electrolytes">Sodium in electrolyte physiology</a></article>
    <article><h3>Potassium, K<sup>+</sup></h3><p>The principal intracellular cation. Potassium gradients are fundamental to membrane potential and normal nerve and muscle-cell function.</p><a href="/pages/electrolytes">Potassium in electrolyte physiology</a></article>
    <article><h3>Chloride, Cl<sup>−</sup></h3><p>A major extracellular anion that commonly accompanies sodium and contributes to electroneutrality, osmotic balance and transport processes.</p><a href="/pages/electrolytes">Chloride in electrolyte physiology</a></article>
    <article><h3>Bicarbonate, HCO<sub>3</sub><sup>−</sup></h3><p>A major component of the extracellular carbon dioxide/bicarbonate buffering system and an important participant in acid-base physiology.</p><a href="/pages/why-bicarbonate">Why bicarbonate</a></article>
  </div>
  <div class="bhd-page__feature" style="margin-top:1.35rem">
    <div>
      <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Citrate</p>
      <h2>Citrate is the fifth species in our product story</h2>
      <p>Citric acid is included in the current formula for acidity and flavour and, once dissolved, participates in acid-base equilibria that produce citrate-containing species according to the drink’s pH. Citrate is also a normal metabolite in human biochemistry.</p>
      <p>For product communication, we describe sodium, potassium, chloride, bicarbonate and citrate as the five highlighted electrolyte species in the prepared formula. This describes the formula’s solution chemistry, not a formal claim that all five are essential dietary minerals.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="bhd-page__split">
  <div class="bhd-page__split-media">
    <img src="https://images.pexels.com/photos/806457/pexels-photo-806457.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&w=1400" alt="Person running outdoors in warm conditions" width="1400" height="950" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
  </div>
  <div class="bhd-page__split-copy">
    <p class="bhd-page__kicker">Why context matters</p>
    <h2>An electrolyte panel is not a universal replacement prescription</h2>
    <p>Exercise, heat, gastrointestinal losses, diet, kidney function and health status can all change the relevance of particular ions. Sweat, for example, contains much more sodium and chloride than potassium, while ordinary meals can provide substantial amounts of several electrolytes.</p>
    <p>That means a useful hydration product should be evaluated in context: the quantity of each ingredient, the amount of water used, the reason for drinking it and the rest of the person’s diet all matter more than simply counting electrolyte names.</p>
    <a class="bhd-page__text-link" href="/pages/heat-high-sweat">Hydration in heat &amp; high sweat <span aria-hidden="true">→</span></a>
  </div>
</section>

